{"name":"Anode","hex":"#89a4cd","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"anode","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An electrode, of a cell or other electrically polarized device, through which a positive current of electricity flows inwards (and thus, electrons flow outwards). It can have either a positive or a negative voltage with respect to cathode of the same polarized device (depending on whether the device is a load or a source, respectively).","(chemistry)  The electrode at which chemical oxidation of anions takes place, usually resulting in the erosion of metal from the electrode."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/anode"}],"entries":[{"title":"Anode","blocks":[{"text":"An anode usually is an electrode of a polarized electrical device through which conventional current enters the device. This contrasts with a cathode, which is usually an electrode of the device through which conventional current leaves the device. A common mnemonic is ACID, for Anode Current Into Device. The direction of conventional current in a circuit is opposite to the direction of electron flow, so electrons flow from the anode of a galvanic cell, into an outside or external circuit connected to the cell. For example, the end of a household battery marked with a + is the cathode."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Zinc_anode_2.svg/330px-Zinc_anode_2.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anode","wikidata":"Q181232"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Fig_bat_Discharge_Charge3.png/330px-Fig_bat_Discharge_Charge3.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Fig_bat_Discharge_Charge3.png/330px-Fig_bat_Discharge_Charge3.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Electric current and electrons directions for a secondary battery during discharge and charge","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFig_bat_Discharge_Charge3.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Charge_discharge_secondary_batterie.png/330px-Charge_discharge_secondary_batterie.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Charge_discharge_secondary_batterie.png/330px-Charge_discharge_secondary_batterie.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Positive and negative electrode vs. anode and cathode for a secondary battery","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACharge_discharge_secondary_batterie.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Anodes-on-jacket.jpg/500px-Anodes-on-jacket.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Anodes-on-jacket.jpg/500px-Anodes-on-jacket.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sacrificial anodes mounted on the fly for corrosion protection of a metal structure","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAnodes-on-jacket.jpg"}],"sounds":{"anode":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e1/En-us-anode.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/e/e1/En-us-anode.ogg/En-us-anode.ogg.mp3"}]},"pictures":[{"title":"Anode sacrificielle","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Anode_sacrificielle.jpg/500px-Anode_sacrificielle.jpg","ratio":0.8234295415959253,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anode_sacrificielle.jpg","share":0.19375},{"title":"Galvanic-anode hg","image":"https://thumb.wikimedi